  "notes": "Chechen in Cyrillic script. Rich ejective series: КӀ, ПӀ, ТӀ, ЦӀ, ЧӀ. ГӀ = pharyngeal /ʕ/; ХЬ = pharyngeal /ħ/; ХӀ = /h/. Front rounded vowels: УЬ = /y/, ОЬ = /wø/, ЭЬ = /ø/. Pharyngeals and ejectives are typologically notable. Complex consonant clusters. Stress exemption: Chechen word stress is non-contrastive (typically realized near the start of the word) and is not marked by or reliably predictable from the Cyrillic orthography modeled here; no stress block is declared. Source: Chechen language, Wikipedia. Open question: the consonant chart in Dotton & Wagner (p. 5) has a uvular series /q qʼ qː/ and a voiced dorsal fricative /ʁ/ that no grapheme here produces, while ⟨гӀ⟩ is mapped to the pharyngeal /ʕ/.",
